{"qid":"Q5724","names":["Anurādhapura"],"title":"Anurādhapura","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Anuradhapura Kingdom was the dominant polity of ancient Sri Lanka, with its capital at Anuradhapura in the dry-zone north, traditionally dated from the reign of Pandukabhaya (437–367 BCE) and continuing for over a millennium. Buddhism arrived under King Devanampiya Tissa (r. c. 250–210 BCE) when the missionary Mahinda, son of Ashoka, converted the court; the Mahavihara monastery became the authoritative seat of Theravada orthodoxy and its chronicles (Dipavamsa, Mahavamsa) are primary historical sources. The kingdom periodically fell to South Indian Chola and Pandya invasions; the final sack by the Chola king Rajaraja I c. 993 CE forced the Sinhalese court to abandon Anuradhapura for Polonnaruwa.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The capital city is archaeologically well documented; the kingdom's territorial extent fluctuated dramatically with South Indian invasions and is coarsely reconstructed for most of its duration.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Anuradhapura","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Anuradhapura"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Anuradhapura Kingdom","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anuradhapura_kingdom"},{"label":"Britannica: Sri Lanka — Ancient Ceylon","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Sri-Lanka/Ancient-Ceylon"}],"britannica":"place/Anuradhapura-Sri-Lanka","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Anurādhapura","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Mutasiva","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":-367,"end":-307,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Devanampiya Tissa",